## Summary

Implements and executes the preregistered W6 campaign from #55 on top of merged W6-00.

### Frozen design

- discovery: 6 fresh Fields / 72 agents / 3 home→host routes;
- post-discovery replication: 6 additional Fields / 72 agents / 3 new routes;
- 128 service trials/window;
- 12 destination-learning episodes;
- 24-episode source-recovery cap;
- W1 probability law reused unchanged (0.38 / 0.14 / 0.90);
- one migrant per route for W6-01–05;
- two public-selected members, 12 formation episodes, 128 paired trials, 1-bit communication for W6-06;
- strict ±2 pp effect/equivalence band.

## Result

Final synthesis: **`w6_discovery_not_replicated`**.

The overall gate fails only because W6-06 does not satisfy the frozen unseen route-consistency requirement. The individual mobility findings replicate.

### W6-01 — Secondment accounting

- discovery mean source loss: **+1.5721 pp**; host gain: **+0.6499 pp**; world-total change **−0.9222 pp**;
- replication mean source loss: **+2.5046 pp**; host gain: **+2.2229 pp**; world-total change **−0.2818 pp**.

Mobility can impose a real source opportunity cost. Host benefit is heterogeneous and does not automatically dominate source loss.

### W6-02 — Mobility-mode leakage control: PASS

Secondment and temporary migration produced exactly identical first-window home/host service outputs on all six routes across both phases. Lifecycle metadata did not leak into the outcome law.

### W6-03 — Permanent migration / brain drain: REPLICATED NEGATIVE PERSISTENT-LOSS CLASSIFICATION

- discovery post-recovery persistent source loss: **−5.2365 pp**, negative on 3/3 routes;
- replication: **−4.8514 pp**, negative on 3/3 routes;
- all routes recovered within the 24-episode local curriculum cap;
- mean replacement latency: **3.67 episodes discovery / 3.00 replication**;
- classification gate: **PASS**.

The source does experience short-run absence cost, but the fixed local recovery mechanism removed it and ultimately raised the evaluated source frontier above its own pre-move baseline. Persistent brain drain is therefore **not supported** under this recovery mechanism.

### W6-04 — Returned learning: REPLICATED POSITIVE

Both return arms received the same 12 destination-learning episodes; only whether acquired agent-owned state was carried home differed.

- discovery returned-learning effect: **+3.0377 pp**, positive 3/3 routes;
- replication: **+4.2531 pp**, positive 3/3 routes;
- classification gate: **PASS**.

Supported claim: provenance-bearing destination-acquired agent-owned learning can return and improve source service capability.

### W6-05 — Brain circulation: SUPPORTED IN BOTH PHASES

The preregistered circulation gate is satisfied in discovery and replication: returned-learning effect exceeds +2 pp, at least 2/3 routes are positive (actually 3/3), and learned-return home performance is not below its own pre-move baseline.

This is a brain-circulation result under the explicit curriculum/return contract, not a claim that all migration routes are world-positive.

### W6-06 — PairModule mobility: NOT ROUTE-GENERAL

- discovery intact-vs-reset effect: **+15.3646 pp**, positive 2/3 routes;
- replication pooled effect: **+7.0313 pp** but route effects **0 / +21.0938 / 0 pp**;
- only **1/3** unseen routes is positive;
- frozen route-consistency gate requires at least 2/3 positive;
- gate: **FAIL**.

The threshold/route rule is not changed. W6 does not establish a route-general advantage for moving an intact PairModule, despite a large effect on one unseen route.

## Causal boundaries

